### Commands
Conda environment was activated to provide the necessary software environment for running bwa-mem2, samtools, and MultiQC.

```
conda activate snps
```

A shell script was created for each sample using the trimmed FASTQ files to run bwa-mem2 mem, aligning the reads to the reference genome and piping the output directly into samtools sort to generate a sorted BAM file (.sorted.bam). Below is an example using Sample_A; this was repeated for the remaining samples B-D.

```
Nano Sample_A_R.sh

bwa-mem2 mem \
-t 2 \
sacCer3.fa \
Sample_A_R1.trimmed.fastq.gz \
Sample_A_R2.trimmed.fastq.gz \
| \
samtools sort \
-@ 2 \
-o Sample_A_R.sorted.bam \

Bash Sample_A_R.sh
```

The script loops through the BAM files to evaluate alignment success by running three samtools commands: stats (for detailed alignment metrics), flagstat (for a quick summary of mapped versus unmapped reads), and coverage (to check genomic depth). 

```
for bamfile in *bam
do
	# The ${VAR%pattern} syntax removes "pattern" from the END of the value.
	SAMPLE=${bamfile%.sorted.bam}
	echo $SAMPLE

	samtools stats -@ 2 $bamfile > $SAMPLE.stats.txt
	samtools flagstat -@ 2 $bamfile > $SAMPLE.flagstat.txt
	samtools coverage $bamfile > $SAMPLE.coverage.txt
```

Each command generates a separate text report per sample that are aggregated by multiqc into a quality control report. 
```
multiqc .
```

The resulting report is then transferred to my local laptop directory using secure copy (scp) for viewing.
```
scp apenicks@sphinx.ag.utk.edu:/lustre/isaac24/proj/UTK0505/apenicks/test2/analyses/04_bwamem2/multiqc_report.html .
```

